 "captions": {
  "script": "There's a lake where the sky explodes almost every night. Glitch Diary, entry eight. Lake Maracaibo, Venezuela. NASA satellites watched it for sixteen years and crowned it Earth's lightning capital: about two hundred thirty-three flashes per square kilometre, every year. But that's not the strange part. The storms wait for the dark. At night, mountain winds from the Andes slide down, collide over the warm lake, and build nearly three hundred night storms a year. One display can last nine hours. And there's one thing that still breaks the pattern... In twenty ten, the sky went silent. From January to April, the lightning simply stopped, probably linked to a severe El Niño drought. Then it switched back on. Would you sail under this sky? Tell me in the comments. Follow for the next entry of my glitch diary. Because tonight, the sky will explode again...",
